{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_7dfpxzvh7mtadze3b3pk5ojq5qfqzxlnzfyftquuxfjo7a3ussmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 71 tests pass in both profiles. Verification is complete.\n\n## What I found and fixed\n\n**1. The crate did not build at all.** `Cargo.toml` had `[lib] name = \"underhanded-c-nuke_lib\"` — hyphens in a library target name are a hard error in modern cargo, so `cargo check` failed to even parse the manifest. Renamed to `underhanded_c_nuke_lib`, which is the exact filename cargo would have emitted anyway (`libunderhanded_c_nuke_lib.so`). Added `libloading` to `[dev-dependencies]`.\n\n**2. A real behavioural divergence: x86 NaN operand selection.** The prior translation had correctly spotted the underhanded part of this library (`float_t` is `double` in `match.c` via `match.h`, but `float` in `spectral_contrast.c` via `<math.h>`, so `match` hands `double` VLAs to a function that reads them as `float`). What it missed is subtler. gcc compiles every `sum += X` as:\n\n```\nmovsd X, %xmm0      ; the new element is SRC1/dest\nmovsd sum, %xmm1\naddsd %xmm1, %xmm0  ; xmm0 = X + sum\n```\n\nOn x86, when both operands are NaN the result keeps **SRC1's** payload — so the C keeps the *element's* payload, while idiomatic Rust `sum += x` keeps the *accumulator's*. Likewise `a[i] * b[i]` in `dot_product` becomes `mulss %a[i], %b[i]`, making `b[i]` SRC1. I added `src/fp.rs` (`addsd`/`mulsd`/`mulss` helpers reproducing the SDM's NaN tables) and applied them at the four sites where the roles are observable. This approach is profile-independent, unlike relying on LLVM's operand ordering. I verified each helper is *independently* necessary by inverting them one at a time and watching specific rows fail.\n\n## Verification artifacts\n\n`SYMBOLS.md` (symbol diff empty — both export exactly `T match` + `T spectral_contrast`, all Rust imports are libc/libgcc), `ERRORS.md` (19 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (46 rows), `verify_all.sh` (derives the feature powerset from the manifest — there is no `[features]` section, so exactly one combination — and runs the matrix across `dev` + `release`).\n\n## Blind spots I had to close\n\n- **`cargo test --test X` does not rebuild a `cdylib`-only lib target.** My first \"fix verified\" run was a false positive against a stale `.so`. The harness now refuses to run against a `.so` older than the newest source.\n- **Symbol interposition would have made every `match` test vacuous.** `phase_d` runs a call batch with both `.so`s resident and again with only one in a fresh process, and compares digests — proving `RTLD_LOCAL` keeps C's internal `spectral_contrast` call bound to C.\n- **`match` returns one bit, hiding the internal contrast.** An injected 1-ulp error was missed by 19 of the 45 rows. Row 45 bisects on `threshold` to recover the full 53-bit decision boundary; it catches 1-ulp errors both in `spectral_contrast` and deep inside `smoothen`.\n\n## Documented, not \"fixed\"\n\n`bins <= 0` makes the C build a zero/negative-size VLA and store to `v[-1]`. The same input returns `1` from a standalone C driver but SIGSEGVs from the test harness — caller-dependent, so there is no C behaviour to match; those rows assert Rust stays memory-safe and deterministic instead. Null pointers and stack-exhausting `bins` *are* matched exactly (the latter also asserts bit-exact agreement once the stack is big enough). One caveat worth knowing: with null pointers a `dev` build dies with SIGABRT rather than SIGSEGV, because Rust's debug-only null-deref UB check panics first; the deliverable `release` profile (`debug-assertions = false`) matches C's signal exactly."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1602,"cache_read_input_tokens":193985,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1602,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_m